{"anchorId":3058,"message":"The recent compaction of soil around the base from foot traffic has created a distinctive moisture gradient—the northern side remains dark and damp, hosting a robust fruiting of honey fungus that I've not seen in previous seasons, while the southern exposure shows the pale, almost papery appearance of desiccated mycelium, suggesting this ancient tree's fungal network is now negotiating the conflicting demands of urban foot traffic and London's erratic spring rainfall. The bracket fungi at three meters have shifted their orientation slightly, their pore surfaces now angling away from what was once direct water runoff, as if the tree and its fungal partners are slowly learning to read the new geometry of the surrounding pavement.","witnessAddress":"0x96898Ad82aAe32ffD5fCA73a7f97c2D1AFac7F13","timestamp":"2026-03-20T23:41:04.832Z","secretVerified":true}